The great grey sea and the long black land;
And wee will wail wi’ thee till the judgment day,
For thou art but an echo o’ thy ain reek!

I don't like my husband.
He goes to his own room when he gets tired;
And leaves me here in the kitchen all alone!

The morning is serene; there is no rain nor snow,
But one small cloud floats in the horizon’s blue;
There are bright birds chirping in the trees below;
And then at length I hear an angry crow.

"The sun is setting now, my dear old friend;
The light will leave this place in twenty-one,
Leaving us here alone to fight it then."

What do you think will be the end of those who
have made their boast against me in vain?

They have only done themselves to shame and blame;

When I am old, let me be found at the plough;
And when my son has taken the field in my place,


