"Oh, but you do not know what poverty does!
You have been sheltered from its cruel blows;
But it may come on me before long;
And if it should—God knows how I shall bear it!"
I look into her face so full of woe;
"My dear," I said, "though others must despair,
There are some who would never turn from you,
For you have in your heart such gentle truth,
Such love for all poor human beings too."

The Great Unmoved mover moves us still :
Through every heart that beats a breath replies;
Nor can this heart be silent when she wills;
She makes me speak of her—I am hers!

“To the Unknown Poetess” (1904), W. B. Yeats

I PASS’D where children play;
 I pass’d where music ran;
 It brought me back my day,—
 I heard it sing again!
 The words that now I hear are like those they sung before;
 But all the meaning seemed to have changed with each new morning.

 In passing thus the past,
 My footsteps came to rest
 Upon what had been fast
 For many an ill-spent year.
 I found not any change that might move me once again to grieve;
 To sorrow was indeed quite unknown that which I left behind.
